 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "My Samsung dishwasher DW60M6050FS keeps showing 4C and beeping, won't do anything"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "The usual causes are a closed or partly closed isolation tap, low/no water supply, or a kinked inlet hose; less commonly the small inlet filter or water inlet valve is restricted. Check that the dishwasher water tap under the sink is fully open and that the inlet hose is not bent, crushed or trapped behind the machine. If the sink’s cold tap also has poor flow, this is a plumbing supply issue rather than the dishwasher."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I looked under the sink. The dishwasher valve is the screwdriver-slot type, and the slot is at 90 degrees to the pipe. The hose has no kinks or bends that I can see, and the water pressure at the kitchen tap is fine. Is that slot position right?"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "No — on that quarter-turn isolation valve, a slot at 90° to the pipe normally means the water is off. The slot should run parallel with the pipe when it is open. Turn it carefully by 90° with a suitable flat screwdriver, then start a programme again; the 4C warning should clear once it can fill."
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "I turned the valve so the slot is parallel with the pipe and started a programme. It's filling now and running normally, the 4C has gone. Thanks!"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "That has resolved it: the isolation valve had been shut, so the dishwasher could not take in water and displayed 4C. Leave the slot parallel with the pipe for the valve to remain open. If it is ever moved again, that is the position to use. Is there anything else you need help with?"
  }
 ],
